WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.463
Why does this chapter have
an end date built in?

2
00:00:02.463 --> 00:00:07.074
Because a group managed service account
only works while A D is in charge.

3
00:00:07.074 --> 00:00:12.083
The program decides early whether A D is
retired or kept as a dated Kerberos island,

4
00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:16.134
and the risk register is blunt:
anything still running under one of

5
00:00:16.134 --> 00:00:20.700
these accounts loses its credential
when the last domain controller goes.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.012
So converting isn't the finish line.

7
00:00:23.012 --> 00:00:26.859
Every account must end up
moving to a workload identity,

8
00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:29.200
retiring, or on a dated island list.

9
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.575
The program adds rules of its
own, labeled as design guidance.

10
00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:36.654
Each account's disposition
isn't decided here:

11
00:00:36.654 --> 00:00:40.463
it's the owner's decision from
Chapter 3, read from that run.

12
00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:43.580
And the strictest rule
is simple: never delete.
